[Music]  this is your Tech news briefing for  Tuesday November 8th I'm Zoe Thomas for  The Wall Street Journal since taking  over Twitter Elon Musk has reiterated  his idea that the platform should be a  global Town Square to share ideas but  what ideas get shared or promoted could  be a problem not just for advertisers on  Twitter but for musk's other businesses  Tesla for example relies heavily on  China a place where speech is often  tightly restricted and where the  government often tries to control the  narrative about it outside its borders  as well so how could musk's new  ownership of Twitter affect his EV  company and its ties to Beijing  joining us to discuss that is Stephen  Wilmot you're a editor for the Wall  Street journals heard on the street  column hi Stephen thanks for joining us  hi there  so Twitter is officially blocked in  China but it is used by some companies  and individuals who can find some  workarounds can you lay out what  beijing's view of Twitter is and how  much it cares about what is said about  it well they take a dubious view to  Twitter just like they do to other US  tech platforms who are mostly banned in  China and obviously the whole idea of  being able to express your opinions very  openly is a bit inimicable to beijing's  authoritarian Tendencies and I would say  the other way in which they have a  relationship with Twitter as it were is  that they've been accused of producing  content to influence U.S elections and  so forth so okay so let's talk about  some of the broader connections between  musk and China and one of the big ones  being Tesla can you talk about how much  Tesla needs China  is really important to Tesla it's hard  to overstate how important it is even in  the context of the Auto industry work  China is the largest market and it's  been the largest profit pool for a long  time and Tesla makes about a quarter of  its revenues in China but that's in a  way not the main source of dependency  the main source of dependencies in  production so it's Shanghai factories by  far its biggest production base and it's  also its hub for Global exports Tesla is  trying to diversify away from its  Reliance on China a bit by building up  production in Germany and More in Texas  at this point about 40 of its production  capacities in China how difficult is it  to diversify away from China I mean you  mentioned it's working on factories in  Germany and Texas but is that a quick  process is that one that's really going  to separate it a lot from Beijing no  it's going to take time and one of the  other facets is it's not just production  like final assembly of cars but it's  also the whole supply chain and in  common with the rest of the EV industry  and this isn't a Tesla specific thing  but the whole EV industry has a huge  dependence on China because of the  battery supply chain because of China's  industrial policy and its subsidies for  EVS and this is the problem here that  you know much of Elon musk's wealth is  tied up in this company over which China  has a lot of Leverage and I guess then  the question is what does that leverage  mean for Tesla if  something happens on Twitter that  Beijing disapproves of are they going to  hold that against Tesla in some way I  mean that's a really interesting  question  a whole battery supply chain kind of  being tied up with China so other  electric vehicle makers and also you  know companies like apple making their  iPhones there how have they negotiated  dealing with Beijing and making sure the  Chinese government stays happy while  also being global companies because I  guess it's worth wondering how that  worked for companies that aren't social  media companies that aren't dealing with  content moderation  sure I mean most companies just try and  keep very quiet politically apple is  obviously another us company that's very  dependent on China because of its Supply  chains and Tim Cook the CEO of Apple  tends not to make big political  statements tries to satisfy all sides  it's the same with the established car  industry VW BMW they have a big  dependence on China if they ever make  any kind of political statement it tends  to be very generic like they support  global trade something like that and  Elon Musk tends to do things differently  for better or worse and you know he's  used political statements to really  ingratiate himself with the regime in  Beijing most recently over Taiwan so  we've talked about Taiwan finding some  accommodation with Beijing anyway  Beijing loved it Taiwan less so and he's  actually done the same with Putin in  Russia last month but Russia's a less  important market so he has this  interesting record of ingratiating  himself somehow with these authoritarian  regimes that might also like build a  luxury cars whilst also playing the kind  of White Knight for free speech in the  US and although he's managed to kind of  walk that line successfully so far it  gets a lot harder with this Twitter  purchase because suddenly all of these  the global elements like the  misinformation campaigns that might be  run by Russia or China or Iran on  Twitter they suddenly become his problem  has he said anything about how he might  deal with things like that particularly  say misinformation campaigns I'm not  aware that he said anything I mean at  the moment he's obviously made clear  that he's  going to leave content moderation the  way it is and he's desperately trying to  keep the status quo for now and I think  this is mainly for the benefit of  advertisers at this point rather than  because relations with foreign regimes  have become a flash point the risk  really is I think to Tesla's  Chinese operations I mean the Chinese  regime has a history of discouraging  consumers from purchasing consumer  products when there are geopolitical  tensions it's happened with certain  Japanese companies it's happened with  certain South Korean companies at  certain points so Tesla is particularly  it was always perhaps a risk from rising  geopolitical tensions but it seems like  it's much more at risk now  does it seem like the U.S or other  Western countries might also put  pressure on Twitter or on Tesla given  the relationship that it has with China  absolutely obviously the scrutiny of  social media has only grown in Western  regimes so it certainly doesn't feel  like it will be easy for musk to dial  back any of the  content restrictions in a way that would  you know make it easy I suppose for  pro-chinese voices to be heard or  anything like that he's stuck between  this U.S Paradigm of free speech and the  reality that his Global car  manufacturing Empire relies on an  authoritarian regime in a very large  part and he's trying to juggle so many  balls that you wonder when One's Gonna  drop I guess we'll just have to watch  and see that's Stephen Wilmot Europe  editor for the Wall Street journals  heard on the street column thank you so  much for joining us thanks very much for  having me on the show  and that's it for today's Tech news  briefing if you want more Tech stories  check out our website  wsj.com I'm Zoe Thomas for The Wall  Street Journal  thanks for listening  [Music]

